# Verifact — AI-Powered Testing Platform Implementation Specification

## 1. Mission

You are the principal software architect and implementation agent responsible for designing and building a production-quality, local-first desktop application that becomes the **central testing environment for software projects, developers, and AI coding agents**.

The product will initially focus on PHP and Node.js web applications, but its architecture must be extensible to additional languages, frameworks, testing tools, and execution environments.

The application must unify:

- Automatic project and test discovery.
- Unit, integration, API, functional, and end-to-end test execution.
- An embedded interactive browser.
- Recording manual browser interactions.
- Converting recordings into reproducible automated tests.
- AI-assisted test discovery, authoring, investigation, and maintenance.
- Integration with Codex App Server.
- An MCP server exposing test capabilities to external AI agents.
- Structured execution results and reproducible testing evidence.
- Persistent understanding of application functionality.
- Code-change impact analysis and verification gates.
- Local and distributed test execution.
- Optional collaboration between multiple developers working on the same project.

**The fundamental product principle is that humans and AI agents must use the same testing infrastructure, share a consistent catalogue of tests, and rely on the same verifiable execution results.**

This is not simply a test runner, browser recorder, AI chat interface, or testing dashboard.

It is a **unified testing intelligence platform** that progressively understands the applications it is connected to.

### Primary outcome

A developer should be able to:

1. Launch the application.
2. Select a local project directory.
3. Allow the application to discover the project's structure, technologies, existing tests, and testing configuration.
4. Immediately browse and run available tests.
5. Inspect meaningful results and supporting evidence.
6. Open an integrated browser and manually demonstrate a workflow.
7. Convert that workflow into a reusable automated test.
8. Replay the workflow and confirm its expected behaviour.
9. Ask Codex to investigate failures, write tests, or discover missing coverage.
10. Make all the same functionality available to external Codex sessions through MCP.
11. Optionally connect the project to a shared service so multiple developers can collaborate and compare results.

The application must be useful without AI. AI must enhance the deterministic testing foundation rather than replace it.

---

# 2. Engineering mandate

Implement the application, not merely a specification, proposal, mockup, or proof of concept.

You have authority to inspect the repository, establish a suitable architecture, create modules, add dependencies, run development tools, and implement the required functionality.

If the repository already contains code:

- Inspect it thoroughly before making changes.
- Reuse suitable existing conventions and infrastructure.
- Avoid unnecessary rewrites.
- Identify missing capabilities and integrate the new system coherently.
- Preserve functioning behaviour unless a change is explicitly justified.

If the repository is empty, initialise the complete application.

Use the latest compatible, documented versions of dependencies available in the development environment. Verify APIs against installed source code and authoritative documentation rather than assuming compatibility.

Follow these engineering principles:

- Production-quality Rust.
- Strong module boundaries.
- Explicit state and lifecycle management.
- Minimal unnecessary dependencies.
- Clear error handling and recoverability.
- Secure defaults.
- Testable architecture.
- Incremental implementation.
- Excellent developer experience.
- Consistent component design.
- Stable interfaces between subsystems.
- Minimal duplication.
- No placeholder functionality presented as complete.

Do not build an elaborate system of abstractions before they have concrete consumers.

Prefer small, cohesive, functional vertical slices over implementing dozens of disconnected modules.

Maintain implementation documentation and a progress ledger so that the work can be resumed reliably.

When the full scope cannot be completed in a single execution session, complete the highest-priority working vertical slice, record what remains, and leave the repository buildable.

---

# 3. Core product architecture

The system must separate the user interface, application logic, testing infrastructure, AI integrations, and collaboration services.

A recommended high-level structure:

```text
Desktop Application (GPUI)
        |
        v
Application API / Orchestrator
        |
        +-- Project Registry
        |
        +-- Project Scanner
        |
        +-- Test Catalogue
        |
        +-- Test Execution Engine
        |
        +-- Browser Controller
        |       |
        |       +-- Interactive Browser
        |       +-- Interaction Recorder
        |       +-- Playwright Automation
        |       +-- Evidence Capture
        |
        +-- Test Intelligence
        |       |
        |       +-- Application Knowledge
        |       +-- Coverage Analysis
        |       +-- Test Recommendations
        |       +-- Change Impact Analysis
        |
        +-- AI Integration
        |       |
        |       +-- Codex App Server
        |       +-- MCP Server
        |
        +-- Persistence
        |       |
        |       +-- SQLite
        |       +-- Evidence Artifacts
        |
        +-- Runner Management
                |
                +-- Local Runners
                +-- Remote Runners
                +-- CI Runners
```

The GPUI application must be a client of the testing core.

All major functionality must be accessible without opening the desktop interface.

The core should support:

- Desktop application integration.
- Headless command-line execution.
- Local API access.
- MCP integration.
- Future remote execution.
- Future hosted collaboration.

Do not embed core testing logic directly inside GPUI views.

---

# 4. Technology requirements

## 4.1 Desktop application

Use Rust and GPUI for the primary desktop interface.

Requirements:

- Native application window.
- GPU-accelerated rendering.
- Reusable component system.
- Light and dark themes.
- Persistent navigation state.
- Efficient tables and lists.
- Keyboard shortcuts.
- Accessible interaction patterns where supported.
- Responsive resizing.
- Background work that never blocks rendering.
- Safe handling of application and project shutdown.

Establish reusable design tokens and components before producing large numbers of screens.

Do not assume GPUI provides mature browser embedding, accessibility, or every required native control.

Research and validate the relevant APIs before committing to an implementation.

Where a critical GPUI capability is unavailable, isolate the limitation behind an adapter rather than distorting the application architecture.

## 4.2 Core services

Use Rust for:

- Project management.
- Scanner orchestration.
- Test catalogue management.
- Execution scheduling.
- Process lifecycle management.
- Evidence indexing.
- Persistence.
- MCP server.
- Event distribution.
- Local and remote runner communication.
- Application state.

Use Tokio where asynchronous execution is appropriate.

## 4.3 Browser automation

Use Playwright with a dedicated Node.js worker for browser automation.

The browser subsystem must support:

- Interactive browsing.
- Recording user interactions.
- Running generated tests.
- Screenshots.
- Browser traces.
- Network inspection.
- Console messages.
- DOM snapshots.
- Accessibility-derived element information.
- Isolated browser contexts.
- Test fixtures.
- Authentication state management.

The architecture should preserve Playwright's ability to execute tests independently of the desktop application.

## 4.4 Persistence

Use SQLite for local metadata.

Store large evidence artifacts in a managed filesystem directory rather than embedding all artifacts in the database.

Use schema migrations from the beginning.

Do not store authentication secrets in plaintext.

---

# 5. Project onboarding

The initial application experience should be deliberately simple.

On first launch, show a minimal project selection screen.

Primary action:

**Add Project**

The developer selects a directory containing an application.

No account creation, external service connection, AI configuration, or complex project setup should be required.

Once selected, automatically scan the project.

The project onboarding process should:

1. Validate the directory.
2. Identify repository information.
3. Detect package managers.
4. Detect languages and frameworks.
5. Detect test frameworks.
6. Locate configuration files.
7. Locate candidate test files.
8. Identify test execution commands.
9. Identify environment requirements.
10. Discover supported test cases where safe.
11. Persist a project record.
12. Display the resulting test catalogue.

Provide useful progress information without overwhelming the user with implementation details.

For example:

```text
Scanning Storefront...

✓ Git repository detected
✓ PHP application detected
✓ Composer configuration found
✓ PHPUnit configuration detected
✓ Node.js package detected
✓ Playwright configuration detected
✓ Test directories located

128 tests discovered
3 testing frameworks detected

Project ready.
```

The numbers must come from actual discovery, never fabricated results.

If the scanner cannot fully enumerate tests, distinguish between detected files, estimated candidates, and confirmed individual test cases.

The developer should be able to inspect scan details through an expandable section.

---

# 6. Project scanner

The scanner is foundational.

It must initially work through deterministic filesystem inspection rather than requiring an LLM.

Design it as a plugin-like system of detection adapters.

## 6.1 Scanner capabilities

The scanner must detect:

- Project language.
- Runtime requirements.
- Framework conventions.
- Package managers.
- Test runner dependencies.
- Test configuration files.
- Test directories.
- Individual test candidates.
- Framework-specific test execution commands.
- Runtime environment requirements.
- Monorepo workspaces.
- Nested packages.
- Existing CI testing commands.
- Available coverage configuration.
- Existing test reports where relevant.

The scanner must support incremental rescanning.

Avoid scanning unnecessary directories such as:

```text
node_modules/
vendor/
.git/
dist/
build/
coverage/
.cache/
```

These exclusions are defaults, not absolute rules. Dependency metadata may still need to be inspected through known paths.

Support ignored directories, custom include/exclude patterns, and project-specific overrides.

The scanner must not execute arbitrary project-defined scripts during its passive discovery phase.

## 6.2 PHP detection

Initially recognise:

- PHPUnit.
- Pest.
- Codeception.
- Composer test scripts.
- Custom test commands.
- Common project layouts.

Look for indicators such as:

```text
composer.json
composer.lock
phpunit.xml
phpunit.xml.dist
phpunit.xml.example
tests/
tests/Pest.php
codeception.yml
```

Detect available binaries and Composer scripts where applicable.

Use framework-native discovery where possible.

Support projects with multiple PHPUnit configurations.

Identify tests by fully qualified class/method or another stable framework-specific identifier.

Consider data providers and parameterised tests.

Do not assume that a PHP project uses a modern framework or standard test directory.

Legacy projects must be supported.

## 6.3 Node.js detection

Initially recognise:

- Playwright Test.
- Cypress.
- Vitest.
- Jest.
- Mocha.
- Node's built-in test runner.
- npm, pnpm, Yarn and Bun scripts where applicable.

Detect configuration patterns including:

```text
package.json
package-lock.json
pnpm-lock.yaml
yarn.lock
bun.lock
playwright.config.*
cypress.config.*
vitest.config.*
jest.config.*
```

Locate test files using framework-specific conventions.

Support TypeScript and JavaScript projects.

Respect workspaces and monorepo package boundaries.

Avoid assuming that a script named `test` always runs every available test.

## 6.4 Scanner adapters

Define a stable scanner adapter contract.

Conceptually:

```rust
trait TestScanner {
    fn id(&self) -> &str;

    fn detect(
        &self,
        context: &ProjectContext,
    ) -> DetectionResult;

    fn discover(
        &self,
        context: &ProjectContext,
    ) -> DiscoveryResult;
}
```

This is illustrative, not a required exact API.

Determine suitable synchronous/asynchronous boundaries and structured error types.

Each adapter should provide:

- Detection confidence.
- Supporting evidence.
- Discovered configuration.
- Test identification strategy.
- Supported runner capabilities.
- Known limitations.
- Rescan strategy.

Persist the discovery evidence so developers can inspect why a framework was detected.

---

# 7. Unified test catalogue

After scanning, display a single test inventory.

The developer should not need to understand the underlying testing framework to navigate the catalogue.

Support grouping by:

- Project.
- Package.
- Framework.
- Suite.
- File.
- Feature.
- Tag.
- Execution status.
- AI-generated or manually recorded origin.

Every catalogue entry should include:

```text
Test ID
Project ID
Framework
Suite
Display Name
Source File
Native Test Identifier
Execution Adapter
Tags
Detected Dependencies
Last Execution
Last Result
Historical Reliability
Associated Features
Associated Evidence
```

Use stable identifiers derived from appropriate project and framework metadata.

Do not treat file paths alone as globally unique test identifiers.

Support:

- Search.
- Filtering.
- Sorting.
- Bulk selection.
- Run selected tests.
- Run complete suites.
- Rerun failures.
- Open test source.
- Open test history.
- Inspect associated evidence.
- Refresh test discovery.

A test that has never run must clearly display **Not Run**, not Failed or Passed.

---

# 8. Test execution engine

Create a framework-independent orchestration layer.

The system should invoke existing test runners through appropriate adapters.

Do not replace PHPUnit, Jest, Vitest, Playwright, or other established test frameworks.

The platform should coordinate them.

## 8.1 Execution capabilities

Support:

- Individual tests.
- Selected tests.
- Entire suites.
- Entire projects.
- Parallel execution.
- Configurable concurrency.
- Cancellation.
- Timeouts.
- Real-time output streaming.
- Retries where explicitly configured.
- Environment selection.
- Execution profiles.
- Test history.
- Structured failures.

Each framework adapter must construct commands safely using argument arrays rather than concatenating untrusted shell strings.

Never execute arbitrary shell commands supplied by remote clients without an explicit, controlled execution policy.

Some test frameworks require process-level execution of a group or file rather than a single test. Preserve those constraints transparently.

## 8.2 Result normalisation

Represent results using a common schema:

```json
{
  "run_id": "run-example",
  "test_id": "test-example",
  "status": "passed",
  "duration_ms": 1250,
  "exit_code": 0,
  "environment": "local",
  "artifacts": [],
  "assertions": [],
  "failures": []
}
```

Support statuses including:

```text
queued
preparing
running
passed
failed
skipped
cancelled
timed_out
blocked
infrastructure_error
```

Preserve native framework output alongside normalised results.

Differentiate assertion failures from environmental and infrastructure failures.

## 8.3 Output parsers

Use official machine-readable reporter formats where possible.

Examples:

- PHPUnit JUnit XML.
- Jest JSON.
- Vitest JSON/JUnit where supported.
- Playwright JSON/JUnit.
- Cypress reporter output.
- Mocha JSON/JUnit.

Validate actual runner versions before selecting command options.

Do not rely on terminal output parsing when a stable structured reporter exists.

## 8.4 Runner isolation

Execution can modify databases, filesystems, caches and external services.

Implement clear execution boundaries:

- Project-specific working directories.
- Configurable environment variables.
- Secret redaction.
- Timeouts.
- Process termination.
- Resource limits where supported.
- Runner permissions.
- Explicit handling of destructive test scenarios.

Do not automatically run discovered test commands without the user's approval or an established trusted execution policy.

---

# 9. Test execution evidence

A successful test should create an evidence-backed verification record.

A verification record must identify exactly what was verified.

Store:

- Project identifier.
- Test identifier.
- Execution identifier.
- Result.
- Timestamp.
- Duration.
- Source revision.
- Working-tree state.
- Environment.
- Runtime versions.
- Test configuration.
- Assertions where available.
- Associated logs.
- Screenshots.
- Browser traces.
- Relevant errors.
- Runner identity.
- Initiating actor.

Actors may include:

```text
Human developer
Codex
External MCP client
Scheduled task
CI runner
Remote team member
```

Make records immutable once finalised.

A verification result must not claim that an entire feature works merely because one assertion passed.

The system should communicate precisely:

> These tests passed against this specific source revision, environment and configuration.

Track whether evidence is current, stale, superseded or incompatible with the current working tree.

For shared results, support artifact checksums and tamper detection.

A signed or externally attested execution record can be considered as an extension, but do not misrepresent local execution records as independently trusted attestations.

---

# 10. Embedded browser

The application must provide an integrated browser experience.

This browser is central to manual and AI-assisted functional testing.

The desired interaction is similar to using a normal web browser, but with recording, assertions, evidence capture and replay built into the surrounding interface.

## 10.1 Browser functionality

Provide:

- Address bar.
- Back and forward navigation.
- Refresh.
- Multiple tabs or sessions.
- Browser viewport resizing.
- Device viewport presets.
- Navigation history.
- Console inspection.
- Network inspection.
- Screenshots.
- Session reset.
- Cookie and storage management.
- Recording controls.
- Playback controls.
- Test assertion controls.

The browser should remain usable while test execution and AI analysis occur elsewhere in the application.

## 10.2 GPUI integration feasibility

Before implementing the complete browser interface, establish a working technical spike.

Investigate:

- Native WebView integration.
- Chromium embedding.
- Existing GPUI integration patterns.
- Playwright-driven browser sessions.
- Native window embedding and input forwarding.
- macOS, Linux and Windows compatibility.

The preferred outcome is an actual interactive browser integrated within the desktop application's workspace.

However, do not assume GPUI supports embedded Chromium natively.

Do not fabricate browser functionality using screenshots or a noninteractive preview.

The browser used for recording and automated replay should use a consistent browser engine whenever possible.

If genuine in-window embedding cannot be implemented reliably, provide a clearly identified Playwright-controlled external browser fallback with synchronized recording controls.

Keep browser hosting behind an interface so the implementation can evolve.

Treat browser integration as an early technical risk that must be validated before substantial UI development.

---

# 11. Manual browser recording

This is a primary product feature.

A developer should be able to demonstrate a workflow by using the application normally.

The recorder converts user actions into structured, reviewable testing steps.

## 11.1 Recording workflow

Example:

1. Open a connected project.
2. Open its configured website in the integrated browser.
3. Click **Start Recording**.
4. Log in as a customer.
5. Navigate to a product.
6. Add the product to a basket.
7. Open checkout.
8. Enter customer information.
9. Select delivery.
10. Complete a sandbox order.
11. Observe the confirmation page.
12. Click **Complete Recording**.
13. Review the captured actions.
14. Define or confirm success assertions.
15. Generate an automated test.
16. Replay the test in an isolated browser context.
17. Save the verified test.

The workflow should require minimal configuration.

## 11.2 Recording controls

Support:

```text
Start
Pause
Resume
Stop
Complete
Discard
Add Checkpoint
Add Assertion
Add Note
```

Provide a clear recording indicator.

Display the captured step count and current recording state.

Allow users to rename the recording and edit its description.

Never silently discard a recording after an unexpected application shutdown.

Use incremental persistence or recovery checkpoints.

## 11.3 Captured browser events

Capture meaningful events such as:

- Navigation.
- Clicking.
- Typing.
- Form submission.
- Select changes.
- Checkbox interactions.
- Keyboard actions.
- File uploads.
- Relevant hover interactions.
- Modal interactions.
- Tab changes.
- Relevant network activity.
- Visible state changes.
- Console errors.

Avoid recording every incidental mouse movement or scroll event as a separate testing action.

Normalise raw events into meaningful user actions.

For example:

```text
User typed into email input
```

should become:

```text
Fill customer email
```

rather than numerous character-by-character events.

## 11.4 Structured recording model

Use a framework-independent intermediate representation.

Conceptually:

```json
{
  "recording_id": "recording-example",
  "name": "Customer completes checkout",
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "navigate",
      "target": "/login"
    },
    {
      "type": "fill",
      "target": {
        "role": "textbox",
        "name": "Email"
      },
      "value_ref": "fixtures.customer.email"
    },
    {
      "type": "click",
      "target": {
        "role": "button",
        "name": "Sign in"
      }
    }
  ],
  "assertions": []
}
```

This intermediate representation should not be tied directly to generated Playwright source code.

It should support editing, replay, validation and alternative exporters in future.

---

# 12. Reliable selectors and replay

A major requirement is generating resilient tests.

Do not record browser actions as brittle pixel coordinates or arbitrary DOM paths when semantic targeting is possible.

Prefer selectors in an appropriate order:

1. Accessible role and name.
2. Explicit testing identifiers.
3. Associated form labels.
4. Stable text where appropriate.
5. Stable element attributes.
6. CSS selectors where necessary.
7. Positional selectors only when justified.

Record multiple locator candidates and enough context to disambiguate elements.

Do not assume that the first matching element is correct.

Avoid fixed sleep statements.

Use event-based waiting and explicit conditions.

The replay engine must distinguish:

- Selector not found.
- Multiple ambiguous matches.
- Element not interactable.
- Navigation timeout.
- Assertion failure.
- Network failure.
- Authentication failure.
- Unexpected application state.
- Environmental failure.

Capture useful evidence whenever replay fails.

---

# 13. Assertions and checkpoints

A recorded sequence of clicks is not automatically a meaningful test.

The application must support defining expected outcomes.

## 13.1 Manual assertions

During recording, allow the developer to select an element and define an assertion.

Examples:

- Element is visible.
- Element is hidden.
- Text equals expected value.
- Text contains expected value.
- Input has expected value.
- URL matches expected pattern.
- Element count matches expected value.
- Attribute matches expected value.
- API response satisfies a condition.
- Order confirmation appears.
- Basket becomes empty.

Where an assertion requires application internals, use approved API/database verification adapters.

Never infer a database state from a browser screenshot.

## 13.2 Recording checkpoints

Support checkpoints during long workflows.

Example:

```text
Customer Checkout
├── Customer authenticated
├── Product added to basket
├── Basket total verified
├── Delivery selected
├── Payment completed
└── Order confirmation verified
```

Each checkpoint should be linked to its recorded state and assertions.

Checkpoints make it possible to identify exactly where a later replay diverges.

## 13.3 AI-suggested assertions

After recording, Codex may suggest additional assertions.

For example:

- Basket contains the expected product.
- Quantity is correct.
- Total is calculated correctly.
- Voucher has been applied.
- Duplicate order submission is prevented.
- Confirmation reference is generated.

Clearly distinguish suggested assertions from validated assertions.

AI must not silently turn observed application behaviour into the expected specification.

The developer should approve assertions that establish new business expectations.

---

# 14. Recording-to-test generation

The application must transform verified recordings into ordinary automated test files.

Initially target Playwright Test.

Generated tests should:

- Follow the target project's existing test conventions.
- Reuse fixtures.
- Reuse existing helpers.
- Use semantic selectors.
- Use reliable waits.
- Handle dynamic data.
- Avoid unnecessary duplication.
- Include meaningful assertions.
- Run independently of the desktop application.
- Be human-readable.
- Remain maintainable.

Do not generate a separate login implementation for every recorded scenario if the project already has reusable authentication fixtures.

Where appropriate, identify reusable workflows such as:

```text
Login as customer
Add product to basket
Enter delivery details
Apply voucher
Complete checkout
```

A developer should be able to approve reusable actions before they become shared testing abstractions.

## 14.1 Dynamic data

Handle:

- Unique customer identifiers.
- Dynamic order numbers.
- Generated transaction references.
- Changing timestamps.
- Product availability.
- Session tokens.
- CSRF tokens.
- Test credentials.
- Payment sandbox data.
- Variable prices.

Do not hardcode secrets into generated tests.

Use fixtures, controlled environment variables, secure secret references and appropriate data-generation strategies.

## 14.2 Recording verification

A newly generated test should initially be marked:

**Unverified**

The system must execute it in a fresh browser context.

A successful replay with meaningful assertions can promote it to:

**Verified**

If it fails, preserve the recording and generated source for inspection.

Never silently modify a generated test merely to force it to pass.

---

# 15. AI-assisted test intelligence

Integrate Codex App Server as the primary AI development and investigation interface.

Use its supported APIs and lifecycle rather than relying on scraping terminal output.

The AI system should be capable of:

- Inspecting application structure.
- Understanding test configurations.
- Finding existing tests.
- Suggesting missing coverage.
- Generating unit tests.
- Generating integration tests.
- Converting browser recordings into tests.
- Investigating failing tests.
- Comparing previous executions.
- Identifying flaky tests.
- Proposing selector improvements.
- Analysing code changes.
- Suggesting regression tests.
- Explaining test evidence.

AI must be optional.

The scanner, runner, recorder and result catalogue must function when Codex is unavailable.

## 15.1 Codex project sessions

Support Codex sessions scoped to a selected project.

The application should provide relevant context including:

```text
Project information
Detected frameworks
Test catalogue
Test configuration
Application knowledge
Recorded journeys
Recent failures
Relevant logs
Current Git revision
Previous verification results
```

Avoid supplying the entire repository or execution history for every AI request.

Provide targeted retrieval tools.

## 15.2 AI interaction modes

Provide clear modes:

**Ask**

Ask questions about the project or test results.

**Investigate**

Analyse a failure or suspicious behaviour.

**Generate**

Create candidate test cases and source code.

**Explore**

Use the browser to discover application behaviour and coverage gaps.

**Verify**

Run the relevant tests and evaluate the resulting evidence.

## 15.3 AI safety

AI-generated tests may execute code and mutate application state.

Require clear authorisation boundaries.

Do not permit an AI agent to:

- Execute arbitrary remote shell commands without permission.
- Modify production data without explicit authorisation.
- Perform real financial transactions.
- Delete accounts or data outside an approved test environment.
- Expose credentials through logs.
- Publish sensitive screenshots.
- Modify existing tests merely to suppress failures.
- Ignore verification failures and report success.

Provide cancellation and configurable autonomy limits.

---

# 16. MCP server

The application must include a first-class MCP server.

This allows external Codex sessions, coding agents and compatible developer tools to interact with the same testing infrastructure.

MCP must connect to the underlying testing core, not automate the desktop interface.

## 16.1 Required tools

Implement an initial tool catalogue resembling:

```text
project_list
project_get
project_scan

test_list
test_get
test_run
test_cancel
test_result
test_history
test_evidence

test_recommend
test_create
test_coverage

recording_list
recording_get
recording_generate_test

verification_status
verification_report
```

These names are illustrative. Select a consistent, well-documented final API.

## 16.2 Tool behaviour

External agents should be able to:

1. Identify the active project.
2. Discover supported test frameworks.
3. Find relevant tests.
4. Run tests.
5. Poll or receive completion notifications.
6. Inspect structured results.
7. Retrieve specific failure evidence.
8. Request generation of new tests.
9. Query project verification requirements.
10. Obtain a final verification report.

Prefer small structured responses.

Do not return megabytes of logs as the default response.

Use artifact references and paginated retrieval where appropriate.

## 16.3 Example interaction

Codex changes basket pricing logic.

It requests:

```text
test_recommend(
    project = "storefront",
    changed_files = [...]
)
```

The platform identifies relevant tests.

Codex requests:

```text
test_run(
    project = "storefront",
    tests = [...]
)
```

The testing engine returns an execution identifier.

After completion, Codex receives:

```json
{
  "status": "completed",
  "passed": 18,
  "failed": 0,
  "skipped": 0,
  "verification_id": "verification-example"
}
```

The result must be based on actual execution.

Never fabricate successful results.

## 16.4 Project integration

Provide an easy mechanism for connecting Codex to a selected project.

Where appropriate, generate or update a clearly bounded section of project instructions explaining the testing tools available.

Do not overwrite unrelated `AGENTS.md` instructions.

Support MCP configuration generation for compatible clients.

Store connection configuration separately from secrets.

---

# 17. Application knowledge model

The platform should gradually build an understanding of the application being tested.

Do not implement this as one giant AI-generated text document.

Create a structured, versioned knowledge model.

Potential entities include:

```text
Project
Feature
Route
Component
API Endpoint
User Role
User Journey
Test Case
Recording
Assertion
Execution
Failure
Evidence
Source File
Dependency
```

Relationships might include:

```text
Feature uses Route
Route renders Component
Component calls API
Journey exercises Feature
Test verifies Journey
Test touches Source File
Failure affects Feature
Recording demonstrates Journey
```

Persist relationships and their supporting evidence.

Each discovered fact should indicate its origin:

- Static analysis.
- Test discovery.
- Browser observation.
- Manual annotation.
- Approved business requirement.
- AI inference.

Separate confirmed observations from AI hypotheses.

Maintain timestamps, provenance and confidence where appropriate.

Avoid presenting inferred relationships as guaranteed facts.

---

# 18. Coverage and change impact

The platform should support multiple forms of coverage.

Do not combine fundamentally different measurements into one misleading percentage.

Track coverage dimensions separately:

- Source-code coverage where native tools support it.
- Discovered test coverage.
- Feature coverage.
- User-journey coverage.
- Route coverage.
- API coverage.
- Assertion coverage.
- Recorded versus automated coverage.

Clearly display when coverage information is unavailable or incomplete.

## 18.1 Change impact analysis

When source files change:

1. Detect the modified files.
2. Identify associated tests.
3. Inspect known dependencies.
4. Identify potentially affected features.
5. Recommend relevant tests.
6. Explain the recommendation.
7. Execute selected tests when authorised.
8. Update verification state.

Prioritise deterministic mappings over AI inference.

Use AI to improve recommendations when available.

Never claim all affected tests have been identified unless the supporting analysis justifies it.

---

# 19. Verification gates

Introduce configurable project-level verification policies.

Examples:

```text
All relevant unit tests must pass.

New functionality should include relevant tests.

Checkout changes require checkout regression tests.

Existing failures must be explicitly acknowledged.

Tests must run against the current revision.

Required integration tests cannot be skipped.

Generated tests require approval before merging.
```

Verification gates should be usable by:

- Human developers.
- Codex.
- MCP clients.
- CI systems.
- Future team workflows.

Return explicit states such as:

```text
verified
failed
incomplete
stale
blocked
not_evaluated
```

Do not treat missing test evidence as a successful verification.

A verification report should answer:

- What was tested?
- Which source revision was tested?
- Where was it tested?
- Which assertions passed?
- Which checks failed?
- Which checks were not executed?
- Is the evidence still current?
- Does the project satisfy its configured verification policy?

---

# 20. Browser-based autonomous exploration

Once the deterministic browser recorder and replay engine are reliable, support controlled AI-driven exploration.

The AI can:

- Navigate application routes.
- Discover forms and interactions.
- Identify accessible controls.
- Attempt sensible user journeys.
- Investigate validation.
- Look for navigation problems.
- Explore boundary conditions.
- Identify inconsistent states.
- Suggest new tests.

Do not implement uncontrolled random clicking.

Use a prioritised investigation queue.

Each investigation should include:

```text
Target feature
Reason for investigation
Hypothesis
Known context
Allowed actions
Risk level
Execution budget
Findings
Evidence
Confidence
Recommended next action
```

Provide explicit limits for:

- Number of browser actions.
- Execution duration.
- Token expenditure.
- Network requests where feasible.
- Number of retries.
- Destructive actions.

Investigations must be interruptible.

The application should preserve partial results when interrupted.

---

# 21. Failure analysis

A failing test should lead to useful investigation rather than just a red status icon.

Capture:

- Failed test.
- Failed step.
- Assertion details.
- Stack trace.
- Browser screenshot.
- DOM snapshot.
- Browser trace.
- Console errors.
- Relevant network requests.
- Runner logs.
- Source revision.
- Previous successful result.
- Runtime environment.

When Codex is available, allow it to investigate.

The investigation should determine whether the likely cause is:

- Application defect.
- Incorrect assertion.
- Outdated selector.
- Missing fixture.
- Environment failure.
- Network failure.
- Authentication problem.
- Timing issue.
- Data-dependent behaviour.
- Potentially flaky test.

Distinguish hypotheses from confirmed causes.

Attempt controlled reproduction when permitted.

Provide suggested fixes or regression tests without automatically changing application behaviour.

---

# 22. Team collaboration

The application must be local-first but architecturally capable of connecting to a shared team service.

Avoid making a central server mandatory for personal use.

## 22.1 Local mode

A developer can:

- Add local projects.
- Run local tests.
- Record browser interactions.
- Generate tests.
- Use Codex.
- Connect external AI agents through local MCP.
- Inspect execution history.

All local functionality should work offline except capabilities that inherently require network access.

## 22.2 Team mode

Allow a project to connect to a shared service.

The shared service should support:

- Shared project identities.
- Shared test catalogue metadata.
- Execution history.
- Evidence artifacts.
- Team members.
- Permissions.
- Remote runner registration.
- Test execution requests.
- Project verification policies.
- Findings and investigation history.

Do not assume that different developers have identical filesystem paths.

Map a shared project identity to each developer's local checkout.

## 22.3 Test ownership

Actual test source code should normally live in the application's Git repository.

The shared service stores metadata, evidence and execution records.

Avoid creating a proprietary test format that requires the desktop application to run tests.

Recordings may use an internal intermediate format, but approved automated tests should be exportable as standard framework-native files.

## 22.4 Remote runners

Design a runner protocol that allows:

- Local execution.
- Dedicated team workers.
- CI workers.
- Future containerised execution.

A remote runner should report:

- Identity.
- Supported runtimes.
- Available frameworks.
- Capacity.
- Project compatibility.
- Execution state.
- Results.
- Artifacts.

Authenticate runners individually.

Do not allow untrusted users or agents to execute arbitrary commands on another developer's workstation.

---

# 23. UI and UX requirements

The desktop application should be clean, fast, professional and developer-focused.

The desired experience is closer to a modern IDE combined with a lightweight issue tracker than a conventional testing dashboard.

The interface should prioritise actions over metrics.

Avoid overwhelming the user with:

- Configuration panels.
- Large JSON dumps.
- Constant AI messages.
- Excessive status badges.
- Unnecessary modals.
- Deep navigation.
- Repetitive technical details.

Use progressive disclosure.

Advanced information should be available, but hidden until relevant.

## 23.1 Main navigation

Recommended sections:

```text
Projects
  └── Project
        ├── Overview
        ├── Tests
        ├── Browser
        ├── Recordings
        ├── Findings
        ├── History
        └── Settings
```

Treat this as a starting point, not a mandatory screen count.

Consolidate views where doing so simplifies navigation.

## 23.2 Project overview

Show:

- Project name.
- Project path.
- Detected technologies.
- Last scan.
- Last execution.
- Test counts.
- Current failures.
- Recent activity.
- Suggested investigations.
- Primary action to run tests.

The overview should clearly answer:

> Is this project currently verified, and what requires attention?

Do not display an overall green status if relevant verification is missing or stale.

## 23.3 Test catalogue

Provide:

- Searchable list/table.
- Framework filters.
- Status filters.
- Grouping.
- Multi-selection.
- Run buttons.
- Result history.
- Test source navigation.

Clicking a test should open a detail view, not an unnecessary modal.

## 23.4 Test detail view

Display:

```text
Test name
Current status
Framework
Source location
Last execution
Assertions
Execution history
Failure details
Evidence
Associated feature
Actions
```

Use tabs or collapsible sections to hide large logs and traces.

## 23.5 Browser workspace

The browser should occupy the majority of available space.

Controls should be minimal:

```text
Back | Forward | Refresh | Address

Record | Pause | Checkpoint | Assert | Complete
```

A collapsible side panel should display:

- Captured actions.
- Checkpoints.
- Assertions.
- Notes.
- Recording status.

Developers must be able to use the browser naturally without constantly interacting with the recorder UI.

## 23.6 Recording review

After completing a recording, present a concise summary:

```text
Customer Checkout

18 actions
5 navigations
3 checkpoints
4 assertions

[Preview]
[Replay]
[Generate Test]
[Save Recording]
```

Allow users to edit or remove unnecessary actions before generating code.

## 23.7 Findings

Represent findings like lightweight tickets.

Each finding should contain:

- Title.
- Affected feature.
- Severity.
- Reproduction status.
- Evidence.
- Steps to reproduce.
- Associated tests.
- AI analysis.
- Status.
- History.

Suggested statuses:

```text
Discovered
Investigating
Confirmed
Dismissed
Resolved
Regression Covered
```

Avoid automatically classifying every failed test as a product bug.

## 23.8 AI interface

Provide a contextual Codex panel.

It should know which project, test, recording or finding is selected.

Examples:

```text
Explain this failure.

Generate additional tests for checkout.

What isn't covered by existing tests?

Find tests affected by these changes.

Turn this recording into a reusable Playwright test.

Verify my latest changes.
```

AI conversations should not dominate the main interface.

Keep the selected test, browser or project as the primary workspace.

---

# 24. Background execution and event system

The application must support multiple concurrent operations without freezing the interface.

Examples:

- Project scanning.
- Test execution.
- Browser replay.
- Artifact processing.
- Codex investigation.
- Knowledge updates.
- Remote synchronization.

Implement a structured task/job system.

Jobs should include:

```text
Job ID
Project ID
Job Type
Status
Progress
Start Time
End Time
Initiating Actor
Cancellation Token
Output References
Error Information
```

Support live event streaming to desktop and API clients.

Recover gracefully from worker crashes.

Prevent duplicate execution where it would be unsafe.

Persist job history.

Provide a clear distinction between:

- Queued work.
- Currently executing work.
- Completed work.
- Failed work.
- Cancelled work.

---

# 25. Persistence and data model

Create a versioned database schema.

Suggested entities:

```text
projects
project_scans
project_environments
frameworks
test_suites
test_cases
test_runs
test_results
test_assertions
test_artifacts
browser_sessions
recordings
recording_steps
recording_assertions
features
feature_relationships
findings
investigations
verification_policies
verification_records
ai_sessions
background_jobs
runners
team_connections
```

Refine the schema based on actual implementation needs.

Do not create tables merely to match this list.

Use migrations and referential integrity.

Large logs, videos, screenshots and traces should be stored as managed artifacts with database references.

Implement artifact retention policies and cleanup.

Ensure deleting a project from the application does not silently delete its source repository.

---

# 26. Security and privacy

Treat imported projects, browser content, execution output and MCP requests as potentially untrusted.

Required safeguards:

- No automatic execution during passive scanning.
- Explicit trust boundaries for project-defined commands.
- Secure storage of credentials.
- Log redaction.
- Sensitive-input masking during recording.
- Configurable screenshot redaction.
- Restricted remote execution.
- Authentication for shared services.
- Role-based permissions.
- Audit history for privileged actions.
- Controlled network exposure.
- Protection against path traversal.
- Safe process spawning.
- Execution cancellation.
- Environment isolation.

Browser content and test output must not be allowed to override AI system instructions or execution permissions.

Never capture passwords, session secrets, payment card details or access tokens into generated test source files.

Use sandbox payment providers and disposable test credentials for checkout workflows.

---

# 27. Performance

The application must remain responsive when working with substantial codebases and large test suites.

Design for:

- Thousands of test cases.
- Hundreds of test files.
- Large execution histories.
- Long-running test suites.
- Numerous screenshots and traces.
- Concurrent background activity.
- Incremental rescanning.

Use:

- Virtualised lists where supported.
- Pagination.
- Background parsing.
- Lazy evidence loading.
- Bounded concurrency.
- Incremental state updates.
- Indexed database queries.
- Streaming output rather than unbounded memory buffers.

Avoid loading entire execution histories or artifact collections at startup.

---

# 28. Observability and diagnostics

Provide an internal diagnostics view for investigating issues with the testing platform itself.

Include:

- Scanner activity.
- Active jobs.
- Runner health.
- Browser worker state.
- MCP connection state.
- Codex connection state.
- Database migration version.
- Recent internal errors.
- Process output.
- Application version.

Keep diagnostics separate from normal project testing results.

Platform errors must not be misclassified as failures in the target application.

---

# 29. Development workflow

Implement the application through coherent milestones.

Each milestone must produce a working, verifiable increment.

## Milestone 1 — Foundation

Deliver:

- Rust workspace.
- GPUI application shell.
- Shared core.
- SQLite persistence.
- Project registry.
- Basic background jobs.
- Structured logging.
- Error handling.
- Development tooling.
- Initial test suite.

Acceptance:

The application launches, opens a window and can persist project information.

## Milestone 2 — Project discovery

Deliver:

- Directory selection.
- Project scanner.
- PHP detection.
- Node.js detection.
- Framework adapters.
- Test catalogue.
- Scan history.

Acceptance:

The application scans representative PHP and Node.js repositories and correctly identifies their testing infrastructure.

## Milestone 3 — Test execution

Deliver:

- Runner abstraction.
- Initial PHPUnit adapter.
- Initial Jest/Vitest adapter.
- Structured result parsing.
- Live execution output.
- History.
- Cancellation.
- Failure details.

Acceptance:

Users can run real tests and inspect accurate results from the desktop application.

## Milestone 4 — MCP

Deliver:

- MCP server.
- Project discovery tools.
- Test listing tools.
- Test execution tools.
- Result retrieval.
- Evidence retrieval.
- Integration documentation.

Acceptance:

An external compatible AI agent can discover and execute the same tests as the desktop user.

## Milestone 5 — Browser foundation

Deliver:

- Browser integration feasibility validation.
- Playwright worker.
- Interactive browser.
- Session management.
- Screenshots.
- Console/network evidence.
- Browser lifecycle controls.

Acceptance:

Users can interact with a real website through the browser workflow, with browser state and evidence available to the testing core.

## Milestone 6 — Browser recording

Deliver:

- Recording controls.
- Interaction capture.
- Step normalisation.
- Checkpoints.
- Assertions.
- Recording persistence.
- Recording review.
- Replay engine.

Acceptance:

A developer can manually demonstrate a website journey and successfully replay it.

## Milestone 7 — Test generation

Deliver:

- Recording-to-Playwright conversion.
- Fixture handling.
- Selector validation.
- Test source preview.
- Source file generation.
- Replay verification.

Acceptance:

A recorded workflow becomes a maintainable, independently executable Playwright test.

## Milestone 8 — Codex integration

Deliver:

- Codex App Server integration.
- Project-scoped sessions.
- Test generation.
- Failure investigation.
- AI-assisted recording interpretation.
- Structured tool access.

Acceptance:

Codex can inspect a project's test catalogue, generate appropriate tests and request real execution through the testing core.

## Milestone 9 — Application intelligence

Deliver:

- Feature catalogue.
- Source/test relationships.
- Coverage analysis.
- Change impact recommendations.
- Investigation queue.
- Verification policies.

Acceptance:

The system can explain why particular tests are relevant to a code change and report the verification evidence available.

## Milestone 10 — Collaboration

Deliver:

- Shared service.
- Team project identities.
- Authentication.
- Local runner registration.
- Shared execution history.
- Evidence synchronization.
- Remote execution permissions.

Acceptance:

Two developers can use separate local application instances and safely access shared testing metadata and evidence.

Do not begin a later milestone merely to create placeholder interfaces while foundational functionality is incomplete.

---

# 30. Verification strategy for this application

The testing platform itself must be comprehensively tested.

Create fixture repositories representing:

1. A basic PHP/PHPUnit project.
2. A legacy PHP/PHPUnit project.
3. A PHP/Pest project.
4. A Node.js/Vitest project.
5. A Node.js/Jest project.
6. A Playwright project.
7. A mixed PHP/Node.js project.
8. A monorepo.
9. A project with no tests.
10. A project with malformed configuration.

Test the following:

- Detection correctness.
- Duplicate detection handling.
- Stable test identifiers.
- Command construction.
- Result parsing.
- Cancellation.
- Timeout handling.
- Process crashes.
- Rescanning.
- Artifact persistence.
- Database migration.
- MCP operations.
- Browser recording.
- Replay accuracy.
- Generated test validity.
- Secret redaction.
- Failure classification.

Include a small fixture web application with deterministic user journeys for browser recording and replay.

Run real tests against these fixtures.

Do not rely exclusively on mocks to verify actual framework integration.

---

# 31. UI verification and refinement

Compilation and passing automated tests are not sufficient for a desktop application.

You must launch the application and inspect its rendered interface.

Evaluate:

- Visual hierarchy.
- Navigation clarity.
- Excessive information density.
- Spacing.
- Alignment.
- Typography.
- Empty states.
- Loading states.
- Errors.
- Screen resizing.
- Long test names.
- Large result lists.
- Modal behaviour.
- Keyboard interactions.
- Browser workspace usability.

Capture screenshots when the development environment supports it.

Use actual interaction with the application to verify workflows.

Avoid testing only the happy path.

Prioritise simplicity.

A developer should never need to understand the internal architecture to run a test.

---

# 32. Documentation

Create and maintain:

```text
README.md
ARCHITECTURE.md
DEVELOPMENT.md
TESTING.md
MCP.md
SECURITY.md
IMPLEMENTATION_STATUS.md
```

Keep documentation consistent with the actual implementation.

The README should be engaging and useful rather than an enormous technical specification.

Explain:

- What the application does.
- Why it exists.
- Main features.
- Supported frameworks.
- How to install it.
- How to add a project.
- How to run tests.
- How to record a browser journey.
- How to connect Codex.
- How to connect MCP clients.
- Limitations.
- Contribution workflow.

Include authentic screenshots once the UI exists.

Do not present planned features as already implemented.

---

# 33. Non-negotiable engineering standards

The following must be enforced throughout development:

1. Never fabricate test results.
2. Never report verification without actual execution.
3. Never automatically execute untrusted project scripts during discovery.
4. Never conflate a successful process exit with complete functional correctness.
5. Never embed business logic directly into GPUI views.
6. Never make AI mandatory for deterministic testing functionality.
7. Never store credentials in generated test files.
8. Never silently rewrite tests to make failures disappear.
9. Never treat an AI hypothesis as a confirmed application requirement.
10. Never claim browser embedding works until it has been demonstrated.
11. Never implement remote execution without authentication and permissions.
12. Never make shared results ambiguous about their tested source revision.
13. Never allow the interface to become an overwhelming configuration dashboard.
14. Never duplicate test frameworks unnecessarily.
15. Never leave the repository in a deliberately broken state between completed milestones.

Use production-quality error handling, not pervasive `unwrap()` or `expect()` calls in runtime paths.

Keep implementation cohesive and avoid unnecessary architectural complexity.

---

# 34. Desired end-user experience

The final application should feel natural and efficient.

A typical developer workflow:

> I open the application, select my project and immediately see the tests that already exist.
>
> I can run any test and see whether it passes, why it failed and what evidence was captured.
>
> If I want to test a new feature, I open the built-in browser, click Record, use the website normally and mark the workflow complete.
>
> The application understands the interaction sequence and helps me convert it into a reusable automated test.
>
> I replay the test, confirm the result and save it.
>
> Codex can use the same testing system to generate additional tests, run regression checks and investigate failures.
>
> When I work with other developers, we can share test results and evidence without needing identical local development environments.
>
> The application gradually becomes more knowledgeable about the project, its features, its tests and its historical failures.

The user should not need to decide whether to use PHPUnit, Playwright or an AI agent simply to answer whether a known test passed.

The testing platform should expose an intuitive unified workflow while preserving the underlying framework's capabilities.

---

# 35. Immediate execution instructions

Begin implementation now.

First:

1. Inspect the existing repository and development environment.
2. Identify the operating system, available runtimes, toolchains and dependencies.
3. Review the current GPUI APIs and validate a minimal working application.
4. Investigate embedded-browser feasibility before committing to a browser-hosting strategy.
5. Establish the Rust workspace and initial architecture.
6. Implement the smallest functional vertical slice covering project creation, scanning and initial test discovery.
7. Add fixture projects and automated tests.
8. Launch and visually inspect the application.
9. Continue incrementally through the milestones, validating real behaviour after each substantial change.

Maintain a concise implementation-status file identifying:

- Completed features.
- Verified features.
- Known limitations.
- Outstanding milestones.
- Architectural decisions.
- How to reproduce the current working state.

Do not stop after generating a plan.

Do not generate a visually impressive application full of fake test results and nonfunctional buttons.

Implement working infrastructure first, then build a polished interface around that infrastructure.

**The ultimate objective is a reliable, extensible testing environment that becomes the shared source of testing knowledge and execution evidence for developers, Codex and other AI coding agents.**

